---
id: AP-009
type: rule
family: dynamic-execution
title: new Function() compiles a string into executable code
version: 1

severity: error
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 2

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx, .js, .jsx, .mjs, .cjs]
allowlist: ['**/*.test.ts', '**/*.spec.ts', '**/__tests__/**', '**/explain/**']

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: '\bnew\s+Function\s*\('

related: [AP-008]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

`new Function(body)` is functionally equivalent to `eval(body)` —
it compiles the body string and returns a callable that runs the
compiled code in the global scope. Anything that can flow into the
constructor as a string becomes arbitrary code execution at the
moment the returned function is invoked.

The rule is unconditional: even a literal-string
`new Function('return 1')` should be replaced with a normal
function declaration. The dynamic-string ergonomics are never
worth the audit-cost of explaining "this one is safe" to future
readers.

Replace with: a static function declaration, a `Record<string, () =>
Result>` dispatcher for the "N known operations" case, or a
sandboxed isolate (Node `vm`, QuickJS, Worker) when running
operator-supplied scripting is genuinely part of the product.
